 dict(pmid='', doi='10.1080/07853890.2026.2757496', journal='Annals of medicine', title='Heat stress and air pollution in female reproductive health: from pathological alterations to molecular mechanisms', tier='C', basis='Review / no primary exposure', short='Harbin narrative review: heat stress and air pollution in female reproductive health, ovarian reserve to placentation, oxidative stress mechanisms', design='Narrative review', pm='Ambient air pollution including PM2.5 and PM10 as reported by source studies; exposure metrics heterogeneous', geo='Global / multi-region', endpoint='Ovarian reserve, implantation, live birth, pregnancy loss, preterm birth, hypertensive disorders', affil='First Affiliated Hospital of Harbin Medical University, China; First Affiliated Hospital of Harbin Medical University, China', n='Narrative review of epidemiological, pathological and mechanistic evidence on heat and air pollution in female reproduction. Epidemiology links both to diminished ovarian reserve, lower implantation and live-birth rates, pregnancy loss, preterm birth and hypertensive disorders; pathology to impaired folliculogenesis, defective spiral-artery remodelling and endometrial receptivity; mechanisms to oxidative stress, inflammation, epigenetic reprogramming, apoptosis and autophagy. The authors name heterogeneous exposure assessment and poorly defined susceptible windows as the main limits. Weakness: no search strategy or quantitative synthesis, and heat and PM evidence are merged, so no PM-specific dose-response can be extracted. Relevance: low - but the stated bottleneck, exposure in narrow windows such as peri-implantation, is what personal or residential sensing addresses.', sub=corpus.REPR),
 dict(pmid='', doi='10.1021/acs.est.6c11702', journal='Environmental Science & Technology', title='Dust Storms as Dynamic Multipollutant Exposure Systems: Environmental Transformation, Toxicological Mechanisms, and Health Implications', tier='C', basis='Review / no primary exposure', short='Zhejiang Normal/RCEES review: dust storms as dynamic multipollutant exposure systems - transformation, toxicology, health', design='Narrative review', pm='Mineral dust PM with adsorbed anthropogenic pollutants, metals, microbes and secondary coatings', geo='Global / multi-region', endpoint='Health effects of dust events; toxicological mechanisms', affil='Zhejiang Normal University, China; Research Center for Eco-Environmental Sciences, CAS, China', n='Review reframing dust storms as dynamic multipollutant exposure systems: mineral particles acquire anthropogenic and secondary components during transport, so toxicity reflects coatings, metals and biological content rather than crustal mass alone. It integrates environmental chemistry, exposure science, toxicology and epidemiology to propose a basis for dust-specific health risk assessment. The abstract gives no search method, quantitative synthesis or effect estimates. Weakness: conceptual; the claim that transport-aged dust is more toxic per unit mass is plausible but not quantified here. Relevance: moderate - optical low-cost sensors undercount coarse dust and mass alone misses ageing, so dust events are where calibration and toxicity weighting fail together.', sub=corpus.MECH),
 dict(pmid='43051405', doi='10.1016/j.envres.2026.126231', journal='Environ Res', title='Emerging inhalation risks: Global distribution of airborne algal and cyanobacterial toxins.', tier='C', basis='Review / no primary exposure', short='Ohio State review of 27 studies: aerosolised algal and cyanobacterial toxins - microcystin up to 40 ng/m3, brevetoxin, BMAA', design='Scoping review', pm='Aerosolised harmful-algal-bloom toxins sampled by filters, cascade impactors and impingers', geo='Global / multi-region', endpoint='Toxin detection and concentration in aerosol; inhalation health effects', affil='The Ohio State University, USA; The Ohio State University, USA', n='Review of 27 studies across North America, Europe, Asia and Oceania on aerosolised harmful algal and cyanobacterial bloom toxins. Fourteen toxins were detected in aerosol; microcystin appeared in 41% of studies (marine and freshwater, maximum 40.0 ng/m3), brevetoxin in 37% (all marine), BMAA in 11%. Sampling (filters, cascade impactors, impingers) and analysis (ELISA, LC-MS/MS, LC-HRMS) differ widely; transport of kilometres to hundreds of kilometres inland is reported. Weakness: few studies with incomparable methods, no exposure-response data, and size distributions of toxin-bearing aerosol rarely reported. Relevance: a bioaerosol pathway invisible to mass-based PM monitoring; size-resolved sampling near bloom waters is the missing measurement layer.', sub=corpus.RESP),
 dict(pmid='', doi='10.1007/s44408-026-00170-x', journal='Aerosol and Air Quality Research', title='Scanning Lidar Measurements to Investigate Optical Properties of Lower Tropospheric Aerosols Over Delhi', tier='C', basis='Lab / research instrument', short='Scanning lidar optical properties of lower-tropospheric aerosols over Delhi', design='Metadata only: no abstract in Crossref at issue time', pm='Not recoverable from metadata (scanning lidar aerosol optical properties)', geo='India (Delhi)', endpoint='Metadata only', affil='not stated in record; not stated in record', n='Metadata only: Aerosol and Air Quality Research record (Rathore, Radha, Singh, Mandal) with no abstract available in Crossref, Europe PMC or Semantic Scholar at issue time. The title indicates scanning lidar measurements of the optical properties of lower-tropospheric aerosols over Delhi. No instrument specification, campaign period, retrieval method or result can be stated, and no finding is asserted here. Relevance: potentially relevant to vertical-profile context for surface PM in Delhi, where boundary-layer structure governs how column and surface measurements relate; recheck in the weekly metadata sweep before drawing anything from it.', sub=corpus.SENS),
